Async, Await, Task & Multithreading

Corrected, interview-ready answers for Ashutosh Parmar

Key idea: asynchronous programming is about non-blocking work. It does not automatically mean that a new thread is created.

# A1. What are async and await, and why do we use them?

Interview-ready answer

async and await are used to implement asynchronous programming in C#. They allow an application to wait for an operation without blocking the executing thread. They are mainly useful for I/O-bound operations such as API calls, database queries, network requests, and asynchronous file access.

The async modifier allows a method to use await. The await operator asynchronously waits for a Task to complete and then continues the remaining method.

public async Task<User> GetUserAsync(int id)
{
    return await repository.GetUserAsync(id);
}

Short version to remember

async and await help us perform non-blocking operations. They improve application responsiveness and server scalability, especially during I/O-bound work.

# A2. What is the difference between Task and Task<T>?

Interview-ready answer

Task represents an asynchronous operation that does not return a value. Task<T> represents an asynchronous operation that returns a value of type T.

public async Task SaveUserAsync()
{
    await repository.SaveAsync();
}

public async Task<User> GetUserAsync(int id)
{
    return await repository.GetUserAsync(id);
}

Avoid async void except for event handlers because callers cannot await it and exception handling becomes difficult.

# A3. What is the difference between a Thread and a Task?

Interview-ready answer

A thread is an operating-system-level execution unit. A Task is a higher-level abstraction representing an operation that may complete in the future.

• Threads are more expensive and usually require lower-level management.

• Tasks are managed through the Task Parallel Library and commonly use reusable Thread Pool threads when a thread is required.

• A Task does not always occupy a thread. An asynchronous I/O task can wait for completion without blocking a thread.

Short version to remember

A Thread is an execution resource; a Task represents work. In modern .NET code, Task-based APIs are preferred for most asynchronous and parallel operations.

# A4. Does an asynchronous method always create a new thread?

Interview-ready answer

No. Calling an asynchronous method does not automatically create a new thread. During an incomplete I/O-bound operation, await allows the current thread to return to its caller or the Thread Pool instead of blocking it.

When the operation completes, the continuation may run on the same thread or a different thread, depending on the application type and synchronization context.

Interview phrase: async programming means non-blocking execution, not automatic creation of a new thread.

# A5. What is the difference between CPU-bound and I/O-bound operations?

Corrected interview-ready answer

A CPU-bound operation spends most of its time using the processor to perform calculations or transformations. Examples include large image processing, encryption, complex calculations, and processing a large in-memory data set.

An I/O-bound operation spends most of its time waiting for an external resource. Examples include API calls, database queries, network communication, and asynchronous file reading or writing.

• For I/O-bound work, use naturally asynchronous APIs with await.

• For CPU-bound work in UI applications, Task.Run may move heavy work away from the UI thread.

• In ASP.NET Core, avoid using Task.Run for ordinary request processing because it consumes another Thread Pool thread and generally does not improve scalability.

// I/O-bound
var users = await dbContext.Users.ToListAsync();

// CPU-bound example in a UI application
var report = await Task.Run(() => CalculateReport());

Important correction: 'file processing' can be CPU-bound if the application transforms data. Waiting for an asynchronous file read or write is I/O-bound. Small versus large does not determine the category.

# A6. Why avoid .Result and .Wait()?

Interview-ready answer

.Result and .Wait() synchronously block the current thread until an asynchronous operation completes. In applications with a synchronization context, this can cause a deadlock. Even when no deadlock occurs, blocking wastes threads and reduces responsiveness and scalability.

// Avoid
var user = GetUserAsync().Result;
GetUserAsync().Wait();

// Preferred
var user = await GetUserAsync();

They do not always cause a deadlock, but they always block. Prefer async all the way through the call chain.

# A7. How do you handle exceptions in an asynchronous method?

Interview-ready answer

Place the awaited operation inside a try-catch block. Log useful context and handle the exception when recovery is possible. If it must be rethrown, use throw; to preserve the original stack trace.

public async Task<User> GetUserAsync(int id)
{
    try
    {
        return await repository.GetUserAsync(id);
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        logger.LogError(ex, "Failed to get user {UserId}", id);
        throw;
    }
}

Use throw; rather than throw ex; because throw ex; resets the stack trace.

# Quick Revision. Important points to remember

• async does not automatically create a new thread.

• await does not block a thread while waiting for asynchronous I/O.

• Task returns no result; Task<T> returns a result.

• CPU-bound work uses processor time; I/O-bound work waits for an external resource.

• Use await instead of .Result or .Wait().

• Use try-catch around awaited code and throw; when rethrowing.

• Use async all the way from the controller to the data-access layer.

SOLID Principles Interview Answers

Corrected, interview-ready answers for Ashutosh Parmar

Interview tip: State the principle, describe the problem it prevents, and add one small practical example.

# A1. What are SOLID principles, and why are they used?

Interview-ready answer

SOLID is a set of five object-oriented design principles used to create maintainable, testable, extensible, and loosely coupled code. SOLID stands for Single Responsibility, Open/Closed, Liskov Substitution, Interface Segregation, and Dependency Inversion.

• S - one responsibility or reason to change.

• O - extend behaviour without repeatedly modifying stable code.

• L - a derived type must safely replace its base type.

• I - prefer small, focused interfaces.

• D - depend on abstractions rather than concrete implementations.

# A2. Explain the Single Responsibility Principle

Interview-ready answer

The Single Responsibility Principle states that a class should have one responsibility or one reason to change. In an ASP.NET Core application, a controller should coordinate HTTP requests, a service should handle business logic, a repository should handle database access, and focused notification services should handle email or SMS.

Avoid placing unrelated features in a generic Utility class, because it can become another large class with multiple reasons to change.

# A3. Does SRP mean one method per class?

Interview-ready answer

No. A class may contain multiple methods as long as they support one cohesive responsibility. For example, an EmployeeRepository can contain GetById, Add, Update, and Delete methods because all of them belong to employee data access.

public interface IEmployeeRepository
{
    Employee GetById(int id);
    void Add(Employee employee);
    void Update(Employee employee);
}

# A4. Explain the Open/Closed Principle

Interview-ready answer

The Open/Closed Principle states that software components should be open for extension but closed for modification. New behaviour should commonly be added through new implementations rather than repeatedly changing stable code.

public interface IDiscountStrategy
{
    decimal Calculate(decimal amount);
}

public class PremiumDiscount : IDiscountStrategy
{
    public decimal Calculate(decimal amount) => amount * 0.10m;
}

Using if/else is not automatically an OCP violation. It becomes a concern when stable code must be modified for every predictable new type or behaviour.

# A5. Explain the Liskov Substitution Principle

Interview-ready answer

The Liskov Substitution Principle states that an object of a derived class should be usable in place of its base class without breaking the expected behaviour of the application. A derived class must honour the contract and expectations of the base type.

public class Bird { public virtual void Fly() { } }

public class Penguin : Bird
{
    public override void Fly()
        => throw new NotSupportedException();
}

This violates LSP because Penguin cannot safely replace Bird when callers expect every Bird to fly. A better design is to keep common Bird behaviour in the base type and put Fly() in an IFlyingBird interface implemented only by flying birds.

Important correction: 'A class should not be forced to implement unused methods' describes Interface Segregation, not Liskov Substitution.

# A6. Explain the Interface Segregation Principle

Interview-ready answer

The Interface Segregation Principle states that clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use. Large interfaces should be divided into smaller, focused interfaces so each implementation receives only relevant behaviour.

public interface IWorkable { void Work(); }
public interface IFeedable { void Eat(); }

public class Robot : IWorkable
{
    public void Work() { }
}

A Robot is not forced to implement Eat(), so the interface contract remains meaningful.

# A7. Explain the Dependency Inversion Principle

Interview-ready answer

The Dependency Inversion Principle states that high-level and low-level modules should depend on abstractions rather than directly depending on each other. Abstractions should not depend on implementation details; implementation details should depend on abstractions.

public class EmployeeService
{
    private readonly IEmployeeRepository _repository;

    public EmployeeService(IEmployeeRepository repository)
    {
        _repository = repository;
    }
}

EmployeeService depends on IEmployeeRepository rather than directly depending on SqlEmployeeRepository. The implementation can therefore be replaced without changing the service.

# A8. Dependency Inversion versus Dependency Injection

Interview-ready answer

Dependency Inversion is a design principle that says classes should depend on abstractions. Dependency Injection is a technique that supplies a class's dependencies from outside instead of allowing the class to construct them manually.

• Depending on IEmployeeRepository applies Dependency Inversion.

• Receiving IEmployeeRepository through the constructor applies Dependency Injection.

# A9. Which principle is violated when a service creates its repository?

Interview-ready answer

When a service directly constructs a concrete repository, it primarily violates the Dependency Inversion Principle because the high-level service becomes tightly coupled to a low-level implementation.

Better approach: inject IEmployeeRepository through the service constructor instead of creating SqlEmployeeRepository inside the service.

# A10. How does SOLID improve unit testing and maintainability?

Interview-ready answer

SOLID improves unit testing by separating responsibilities and reducing coupling. When classes depend on abstractions, database repositories and external services can be replaced with mocks, stubs, or fakes, allowing business logic to be tested in isolation.

SOLID improves maintainability because responsibilities and changes are localized to focused classes. New behaviour can often be added through new implementations with fewer modifications to stable code, reducing the risk of affecting unrelated functionality.

• Smaller, cohesive classes are easier to understand and change.

• Mockable dependencies make unit tests faster and more focused.

• Loose coupling makes implementations easier to replace.

• OCP can reduce repeated changes to stable components.

SOLID reduces the impact of changes, but it does not remove the need for integration and regression testing.

# Quick Revision. SOLID in one line

• SRP: one responsibility; OCP: extend with fewer modifications to stable code.

• LSP: a child safely behaves as its parent promises; ISP: use focused interfaces.

• DIP: high-level and low-level modules depend on abstractions.
